course: biology
lesson: 302
title: How owls work: silent wings, turning heads, pellets and owl myths
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An owl is a hunting machine built for the night. Its wing feathers have comb-like front edges and soft fringes, so it glides past you in a barn without a whisper, and the mouse below hears nothing either.
explain: Its huge eyes are fixed tubes that cannot roll, so the owl turns its whole head instead, about three quarters of a circle, thanks to 14 neck bones and clever blood vessels that keep the brain fed.
explain: Its ears are often set at different heights, so it can tell where a squeak comes from both left and right and up and down, and a great grey owl can dive through deep snow onto a vole it has only heard.
explain: Think of an owl pellet on a classroom table: a ball of fur and tiny bones the owl coughed up, not poo. Pull it apart with tweezers and you can count the skulls of the mice and voles it ate.
explain: Stories call owls wise, unlucky or ill when they fly by day, but the real owl is simply a superb hunter.
fact: Owl flight feathers have a comb-like front edge, a soft fringe at the back and a velvety surface, which break the air into tiny swirls and make almost no sound; engineers have copied them for quieter fans and wind turbines.
fact: In many owls the two ears sit at different heights, so a sound reaches each ear at a slightly different time and loudness and the owl can tell up from down as well as left from right.
fact: A great grey owl can plunge through deep snow to grab a vole it has only heard.
fact: An owl can turn its head about 270 degrees, three quarters of a circle, but not all the way round; owls have 14 neck bones, twice as many as we have.
fact: Owl eyes are tube-shaped and fixed, so they cannot roll, and they are so big they fill much of the skull; owls see in light far too dim for us, but not in total darkness.
fact: Owls have extra blood vessels and little pools of blood that keep the brain fed when the neck is twisted, and the main arteries run through wide holes in the neck bones so they are not pinched.
fact: An owl pellet is a ball of fur and bones the owl coughs up, not poo, because owls swallow small prey whole and cannot digest bone and fur; scientists count the skulls inside to learn which small mammals live nearby.
fact: Owls have three eyelids: an upper, a lower and a thin see-through one that sweeps sideways to clean and protect the eye without blocking the view.
fact: Owls are skilled hunters but not especially good at puzzles; crows and parrots do much better. The wise-owl idea comes partly from ancient Athens, where the little owl was the bird of Athena, goddess of wisdom, and appeared on silver coins.
fact: In many places owls were feared as signs of death, and in others they were lucky; really they call to keep their patch and find partners, and farmers put up owl boxes because owls eat lots of rats and mice.
fact: Many owls hunt at dawn and dusk, and the little owl and short-eared owl often hunt by day; owls are not blinded by daylight, because their pupils shrink like ours.
fact: Because its eyes cannot roll, an owl bobs and sways its head to look at a thing from slightly different places, which helps it judge how far away the thing is.
fact: Most owls get nearly all their water from their prey, because the bodies of mice and voles are mostly water, though they do sometimes drink and bathe in hot weather.
exercise: Why can't you hear an owl fly past you? | Its feathers have soft fringes and comb-like edges that hush the air | It holds its breath all the way while it is flying past | It flies only when the wind is loud | It glides along without ever moving its wings at all, even to take off
exercise: What have engineers made by copying owl feathers? | Quieter fans and wind turbines | Louder car horns for busy roads | Faster and lighter racing bicycles | Warmer winter coats for the snow
exercise: Why are the ears of many owls set at different heights? | So the owl can tell if a sound is above or below it | So one ear can sleep in the day while the other one listens | So they can hear two songs at once | Because one ear is for day and one for night
exercise: How does a great grey owl catch a vole under deep snow? | It hears the vole and plunges through the snow | It smells the vole right through the deep snow | It digs a tunnel for hours | It waits for the snow to melt in spring
exercise: How far can an owl turn its head? | About 270 degrees, three quarters of a circle | All the way round, 360 degrees, again and again | About 90 degrees, like us | It cannot turn its head at all
exercise: How many neck bones does an owl have? | 14, twice as many as we have | 7, exactly the same as we have | 2, one at each end of the neck | 33, the same as our whole backbone
exercise: Why does an owl turn its head instead of rolling its eyes? | Its eyes are fixed tubes that cannot roll | Its eyes are much too heavy to roll around | It is too lazy to move its eyes about in its head | Its eyes only work when it moves its whole body
exercise: Why doesn't an owl faint when it twists its neck right round? | Extra vessels and pools of blood keep its brain fed | It holds its breath first | It has no blood in its head at all, only in its body | It faints for a second every time
exercise: Can owls see in total darkness? | No, they see in very dim light but not in none | Yes, they see best of all with no light at all | Yes, their eyes glow like torches | No, owls are blind at night
exercise: What is an owl pellet? | A ball of fur and bones the owl coughs up | An owl's poo, dried hard and white in the sun | An old owl egg that never hatched in the nest | A ball of mud the owl rolls for building a nest
exercise: Why does an owl cough up pellets? | It swallows prey whole and cannot digest fur and bone | It eats soil by mistake | It feeds its chicks with them, as soft warm food for the nest | It is ill after every meal and has to be sick
exercise: How many eyelids does an owl have on each eye? | Three | One | Two | Four
exercise: Why is the owl a sign of wisdom in stories? | The little owl was the bird of Athena, goddess of wisdom, in ancient Athens | Owls really solve puzzles better than any other bird, even crows and parrots | Owls can read in the dark | Owls live longer than any other animal
exercise: Why do farmers put up owl boxes? | Owls eat lots of rats and mice | Owls bring good luck to the crops | Owls guard the farm from hungry foxes | Owls lay big eggs that people eat
exercise: Is an owl out in daylight always ill? | No, some owls hunt by day and hungry parents may too | Yes, daylight blinds all owls | Yes, owls only ever fly in daylight when they are ill and dying | No, but it means that rain is coming very soon
exercise: Where do most owls get their water? | Mostly from the bodies of the prey they eat | Only from puddles, drinking every hour | From dew licked off the leaves in the morning | They never need any water
test: Count them: a pellet holds the skulls of 2 voles and 3 shrews. How many animals did the owl eat? | 5 | 3 | 6 | 10
test: Count them: two owls blink. How many eyelids do they have altogether? | 12 | 4 | 8 | 6
test: Count them: you have 7 neck bones. How many more does an owl have? | 7 more | 14 more | Only 2 more | None, it has the same
test: In Winnie-the-Pooh, Owl is the clever one who knows everything, yet spells his own name «Wol». What is true about real owls? | They are superb hunters but not especially good at puzzles | They are the cleverest of all birds, much cleverer than crows | They can learn to read and write | They are the only birds that can count to ten and beyond
test: «bruv, owls can spin their heads right round like in the cartoon» What is right? | No, about three quarters of a circle at most | Yes, round and round many times without stopping | Yes, but only once a day | No, owls cannot move their heads
test: «gross, we're poking owl poo in science??» What is the best answer? | No, a pellet is coughed-up fur and bones, not poo | Yes, it is owl poo dried in a bag | Yes, owls make little balls of poo to feed their chicks | No, pellets are made in factories for schools
test: What would happen if an owl had flight feathers as stiff and smooth as a pigeon's? | Mice would hear it coming and escape more often | It would see much better at night | It would fly in total silence | It would stop needing to hunt
test: Grandad says an owl calling at night means bad luck is coming. What is true? | It is an old story; the owl calls to keep its patch or find a partner | It is true in every country of the world, as everyone knows | Owls call only before a storm | Owls call only when someone is ill
test: You see a short-eared owl hunting over a field at noon. What is most likely? | It is a kind that often hunts by day, maybe feeding chicks | It is blinded by the sun and has got lost | It must be a ghost owl | It is sleep-walking
test: Why might an owl bob and sway its head when it looks at you? | To judge how far away you are from different views | Because it is dancing to some music only it can hear | Because its neck is aching | Because its eyes roll around inside
test: What would happen to an owl's brain if the arteries in its neck were pinched when it turned? | It could lose blood, which is why owls have wide holes for the arteries | Nothing, because brains need no blood | It would grow a new brain | It would turn its head faster
exam: Identify the principal function of the comb-like leading edge and soft fringes of the flight feathers of owls. | The reduction of the noise of flight by breaking the air into minute swirls | The production of a loud warning sound during flight, by which rivals are deterred | The storage of water for use in hot weather | The attraction of prey by means of a visual display
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the rotation of the head of an owl. | The head may be rotated approximately two hundred and seventy degrees, but not through a complete circle | The head may be rotated without limit through repeated complete circles | The head is incapable of any rotation whatsoever | The head may be rotated only ninety degrees in each direction
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an owl pellet? | A mass of indigestible fur and bone regurgitated by the owl | The faeces of the owl after complete digestion | An unfertilised egg discarded from the nest | A store of food concealed by the owl beneath leaves for the winter
exam: Ascertain the reason for which an owl obtains sufficient water without frequent drinking. | The bodies of its prey consist largely of water | Owls require no water whatever for survival | Owls absorb water through their feathers from the air | Owls drink only once in each season
